guide = heading('PARTICIPANT GUIDE / PRACTICAL WORKFLOW','A complete result in one day.','Use a reliable baseline, a shared development comparison and a small number of experiments you can explain.') + """
<section class="reading"><div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href="#" data-guide aria-disabled="true">Participant guide released at 10:00</a><a class="button outline" href="#" aria-disabled="true" data-drive>Challenge data link forthcoming</a><a class="button outline" href="#" aria-disabled="true" data-starter>Starter released at 10:00</a></div>
<h2>Before kickoff: transfer and environment checks</h2><p>Download, unpack, verify checksums, read technical instructions and check accounts, packages and compute with unrelated synthetic examples. You may use the neutral practice CSV for delivery checks. Challenge-data exploration, derived features, training, tuning and visual insights start at kickoff, including work performed by AI agents.</p>
<h2>Choose a route you can finish</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Useful experiments</th><th>Main practical constraint</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Compact features</td><td>Regularized linear models, suitable trees, local versus context features and controlled helper signals.</td><td>Correct joins, partition-safe preprocessing and an honest common-development comparison.</td></tr><tr><td>Prepared spatial patches</td><td>Local, wider or multiscale models on the 64-channel embedding fields.</td><td>Masks, encoding, focal offsets, GPU memory and measured final inference time.</td></tr><tr><td>National German rasters</td><td>Custom German environmental summaries or spatial exploration.</td><td>148.57 GB of optional data, source transforms and producing features compatible with anonymous final inputs.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Start with the supplied CPU model and a working keyed export after kickoff. Keep a short log of hypothesis, features, split, seed, runtime and result. Change one important choice at a time. Optional five-fold CV helps compare promising models; it is not required and does not earn points on its own.</p>
<h2>Use the early test-inference window</h2><ol><li>Run the common German development comparison and rehearse practice delivery early.</li><li>Develop one or two useful model comparisons and two or three clear visualizations.</li><li>Copy required encrypted test inputs onto local runtime storage before release. Keep a tested table model as a fallback.</li><li>At 13:30 German time, unlock and verify the final inputs, infer every request in the supplied final sample and validate its complete row-ID set.</li><li>Open the Submit tab, sign in with your confirmed team account and upload the prediction CSV. Keep the receipt and check for Accepted. Leave the final-entry checkbox selected, or use “Use for final” to choose an earlier accepted entry. Include your method/code reference and upload your presentation before the deadline.</li></ol>
<h2>Present a useful, defensible finding</h2><p>Good insight questions include observation coverage, source composition, seasonality, coordinate uncertainty, environmental support scale and model errors. Explain the denominator and plausible reporting biases. For cross-species work, compare with and without helper information on identical focal labels, supports and splits. A well-controlled negative result can be valuable.</p><p>Use five minutes for the question, approach, common-development result, two or three findings and limitations. The jury then has two minutes for questions. The presentation form requires a PDF up to 10 MB, with an optional presentation source up to 100 MB. Organizers fix the latest complete valid deck at the work deadline.</p><p><a class="button outline" href="#" aria-disabled="true" data-presentation>Presentation form available when opened</a></p>
<h2>Questions teams often ask</h2>
<h3>Why train on Germany instead of all Europe?</h3><p>The learning and exploration release has a clear German boundary. Final evaluation tests geographic transfer to nearby Central Europe while foreign bird observations and exact evaluation locations stay with the organizers. Every team receives the same permitted data.</p>
<h3>Will a city park disappear in a coarse average?</h3><p>It can. The native vector and 10 m local patch retain finer environmental detail, while context summaries and wider patches describe the landscape. Compare supports rather than assuming one resolution is best. Fine pixels do not remove uncertainty in the reported bird position.</p>
<h3>Are 100 m and 1 km features single coarse pixels?</h3><p>No. They summarize circular neighborhoods of those radii. Native vectors, context summaries, 10 m patches and 40 m patches are different environmental supports; the data schemas explain each representation.</p>
<h3>Can I use an ordinary image model?</h3><p>You can develop spatial architectures, but inputs have 64 environmental channels and validity masks, not RGB colors. Apply documented decoding and normalization. Generic brightness augmentation or ImageNet color preprocessing has no automatic meaning here.</p>
<h3>Must I train five models for CV?</h3><p>No. Everyone reports the common German development result; five supplied spatial folds are optional. Learned preprocessing and helper stages must still obey the partition and exclusion rules.</p>
<h3>Can I refit on training plus development?</h3><p>Yes, after model selection and with a declaration. Retain the earlier honest held-out development score and use the same final inference interface.</p>
<h3>Can I download additional bird observations?</h3><p>No. Additional bird labels, range maps and pretrained bird-distribution predictions are prohibited. Teams may obtain additional environmental data for analysis, visualization and training. Final predictions must be computable from the supplied anonymous test inputs; organizers will not prepare additional test features on request. Use supplied bird observations only. Retrieving external bird observations, species range maps or pretrained bird-distribution predictions, identifying test locations, or retrieving evaluation labels is prohibited.</p>
<h3>Does background zero mean the bird was absent?</h3><p>No. It is a matched recording opportunity sampled for this benchmark. Unreported species remain unknown. Scores rank reported seasonal presence against that fixed background rather than measuring calibrated occupancy.</p>
<h3>How can other species help at final locations?</h3><p>Learn transferable helper signals inside the permitted training partitions and predict them from the supplied final inputs. Observed helper labels at final locations are not supplied. Use out-of-fold or another leakage-safe construction and a matched control.</p>
<h3>What if my scoring receipt arrives after the deadline?</h3><p>The original unedited Hugging Face server receipt binds the exact request and ciphertext fingerprint. Its timestamp controls eligibility, even if scoring finishes later. Keep the receipt, avoid edits and allow time to check acceptance.</p>
<h3>Is the private score selected from my best attempt?</h3><p>No. Final performance uses one chosen accepted prediction; the latest accepted captain upload is the fallback; if only teammates uploaded, the captain must choose an entry. Five accepted final predictions apply across the whole event, not per day.</p>
<h3>Do slide uploads need dataset editing permission?</h3><p>No. The presentation Form routes files to private organizer storage. Challenge data remains a download-only folder for participants. Open it below prediction submission in the Submit tab. It requires a Google account; keep its confirmation. No separate GitHub nomination is required.</p>
<h2>Reproducibility handoff</h2><p>Enter the method/report and exact code/checkpoint link in the Submit tab before your final upload, or before sending a changed choice with “Use for final”. Keep the presentation form confirmation separately.</p><p>Complete the method report with feature choices, seeds, runtime, common-development result, optional CV, AI/API and compute use, permitted-data and preparation declarations, any final refit and the actual CSV SHA256. Provide the exact code/checkpoint reference. Keep source notices with derived work and retain the limitations of annual embeddings, coordinate uncertainty and reported observations.</p><p><a href="rules.html">Read the full rules →</a> · <a href="submit.html">Upload predictions →</a> · <a href="data.html">Data sizes, species and licenses →</a></p></section>"""

